      "offense": "Joint Liability / Common Intention",
      "chapter": "General Explanations",
      "badge": "Key Restructuring",
      "summary": "When a criminal act is done by several persons in furtherance of the common intention of all, each of such persons is liable for that act in the same manner as if it were done by him alone.",
      "changes": "In BNS 2023, joint liability (old S. 34 IPC) and common object of unlawful assembly (old S. 149 IPC) have both been unified under Section 3. Sub-section (5) of Section 3 specifically re-enacts the doctrine of common intention without altering its core elements: prior meeting of minds, pre-arranged plan, and physical or active participation during execution.",
      "cases": [
        "Barendra Kumar Ghosh v. King Emperor (1925)",
        "Mahbub Shah v. King Emperor (1945)",
        "JM Desai v. State of Bombay (1961)"
      ],
      "duExamTip": "In problem questions involving multiple co-accused where only one fired or struck, cite S. 3(5) BNS alongside Mahbub Shah to demonstrate difference between 'common intention' and 'similar intention'."
